WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Welcome. Observability is not collecting
everything. It is answering important

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
operational questions with justified,
privacy-conscious evidence. In this

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000
lesson, you will define questions and
objectives, correlate exact serving

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
identities, minimize sensitive telemetry,
interpret performance distributions and

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.000
cost, and build alerts that lead an
accountable operator toward

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
safe action and recovery. The examples are
synthetic teaching fixtures.

7
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
They are not live infrastructure results,
provider prices, model benchmarks,

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.000
or deployment approval.

9
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.450
Start with questions an operator, product
owner, security reviewer, or

10
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:40.900
incident responder must answer. Is the
approved model and runtime

11
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:45.350
bundle serving? Are users receiving valid
outcomes? Which bounded slices

12
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:49.800
fail? Where is time spent? What is queued,
rejected, canceled,

13
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.250
or retried? Which resource is saturated?
Did an update change

14
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:58.700
quality, policy outcomes, latency, cost,
or recovery? Can an incident

15
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.150
be scoped and reproduced without exposing
unnecessary content? Translate each

16
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.600
question into an indicator and objective.
State the population, observation

17
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.050
window, denominator, threshold, owner,
decision, response, and retention period.

18
00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:16.500
For example, a synthetic
interactive-latency objective can require

19
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.950
at least 95 percent of valid requests to
finish at

20
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:25.400
or below 400 milliseconds in a five-minute
window. The denominator

21
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.850
is part of the contract. Missing
measurements are unknown, not

22
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:34.300
successful outcomes. A dashboard panel
with no owned decision is

23
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:38.750
decorative telemetry. A useful contract
tells an owner what evidence

24
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:43.200
changes a decision, such as tuning,
capacity adjustment, release hold,

25
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.650
or rollback. NIST's generative artificial
intelligence risk profile supports

26
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:52.100
managing and measuring risk across the
lifecycle. Here, that becomes

27
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.550
a practical rule: every signal has an
approved purpose, an

28
00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:01.000
accountable consumer, and a defined
response and retention boundary.

29
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.294
Correlate the serving path with a bounded
application-owned request or

30
00:02:05.294 --> 00:02:09.588
run identifier. Connect gateway, adapter,
inference server, runtime, policy,

31
00:02:09.588 --> 00:02:13.882
optional tool, and user-outcome events.
Record service and instance identity,

32
00:02:13.882 --> 00:02:18.176
exact model and runtime bundle, release,
route, operation, status, bounded

33
00:02:18.176 --> 00:02:22.471
error category, policy disposition, input
and output size measures, queue

34
00:02:22.471 --> 00:02:26.765
time, processing time, resource
observations, and an evaluation or result

35
00:02:26.765 --> 00:02:31.059
reference. OpenTelemetry Semantic
Conventions provide shared names and

36
00:02:31.059 --> 00:02:35.353
stability metadata, but a field exposed by
one runtime is

37
00:02:35.353 --> 00:02:39.647
not proof that another provider exposes an
equivalent field. Version

38
00:02:39.647 --> 00:02:43.941
the core contract you own, then use
explicit adapters for

39
00:02:43.941 --> 00:02:48.235
external conventions. A request identifier
can connect traces and logs,

40
00:02:48.235 --> 00:02:52.529
but it must not become an unbounded metric
label. Do

41
00:02:52.529 --> 00:02:56.824
not use raw personal identifiers, prompts,
or arbitrary user values

42
00:02:56.824 --> 00:03:01.118
as labels. Exact model, tokenizer,
runtime, configuration, adapter,

43
00:03:01.118 --> 00:03:05.412
precision, and release identities let an
operator compare releases and

44
00:03:05.412 --> 00:03:09.706
reproduce failures. A friendly alias alone
can hide that replicas

45
00:03:09.706 --> 00:03:14.000
served different artifacts or policies.

46
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.368
Consider a synthetic assistant request
that feels slow and returns

47
00:03:18.368 --> 00:03:22.737
an invalid citation. The gateway event
shows an approved route

48
00:03:22.737 --> 00:03:27.105
and release with no policy denial. The
adapter records a

49
00:03:27.105 --> 00:03:31.474
bounded input-size class and an output
contract failure. The inference

50
00:03:31.474 --> 00:03:35.842
server records two seconds of queue time
and one second

51
00:03:35.842 --> 00:03:40.211
to first output. Runtime metrics show high
accelerator use but

52
00:03:40.211 --> 00:03:44.579
available host memory. Exact identity
reveals that one replica loaded

53
00:03:44.579 --> 00:03:48.947
the prior prompt-template bundle. A
release annotation shows the mismatch

54
00:03:48.947 --> 00:03:53.316
began during a partial rollout. No raw
prompt is needed

55
00:03:53.316 --> 00:03:57.684
to answer the first questions: which build
failed, where time

56
00:03:57.684 --> 00:04:02.053
accumulated, and which replica should
leave service. An authorized evaluator

57
00:04:02.053 --> 00:04:06.421
can use a separately governed fixture to
reproduce citation quality.

58
00:04:06.421 --> 00:04:10.789
The operator isolates the mismatched
replica, verifies the approved bundle,

59
00:04:10.789 --> 00:04:15.158
repeats readiness and regression checks,
and records recovery. Correlation turned

60
00:04:15.158 --> 00:04:19.526
several signals into a bounded diagnosis
without copying learner content

61
00:04:19.526 --> 00:04:23.895
into every store. Notice the causal
boundary. The evidence supports

62
00:04:23.895 --> 00:04:28.263
a likely release mismatch and a safe
investigation path. It

63
00:04:28.263 --> 00:04:32.632
does not prove that every invalid citation
has the same

64
00:04:32.632 --> 00:04:37.000
cause.

65
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.118
Default to metadata and derived
measurements, not raw prompts, responses,

66
00:04:41.118 --> 00:04:45.235
retrieved documents, credentials,
authorization headers, personal

67
00:04:45.235 --> 00:04:49.353
identifiers, or tool secrets. Classify
every proposed field as required

68
00:04:49.353 --> 00:04:53.471
metadata, derived measurement, optional
sensitive content, or prohibited secret.

69
00:04:53.471 --> 00:04:57.588
For approved content sampling, document
purpose and authority, minimize and

70
00:04:57.588 --> 00:05:01.706
redact, encrypt in transit and storage,
restrict access, audit use,

71
00:05:01.706 --> 00:05:05.824
set short retention, and test deletion.
Sampling reduces volume; it

72
00:05:05.824 --> 00:05:09.941
does not remove sensitivity. Threat-model
instrumentation, collectors, exporters,

73
00:05:09.941 --> 00:05:14.059
queues, stores, dashboards, alerts,
exemplars, support bundles, backups, and

74
00:05:14.059 --> 00:05:18.176
analyst exports. An encrypted inference
request can still be disclosed

75
00:05:18.176 --> 00:05:22.294
by a verbose exception, trace body, metric
label, screenshot, or

76
00:05:22.294 --> 00:05:26.412
copied support archive. Control
cardinality as both a reliability and

77
00:05:26.412 --> 00:05:30.529
privacy concern. Unbounded request, user,
prompt, or document values can

78
00:05:30.529 --> 00:05:34.647
overwhelm metric systems and create
searchable copies. Test rejected requests

79
00:05:34.647 --> 00:05:38.765
and exception paths because they may copy
input content. Detect

80
00:05:38.765 --> 00:05:42.882
unauthorized field appearance with
fixtures and scans, and audit telemetry

81
00:05:42.882 --> 00:05:47.000
access itself.

82
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.250
Checkpoint. Inference transport is
encrypted, but a trace exporter copies

83
00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:55.500
full prompts, retrieved passages,
authorization headers, and responses into

84
00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.750
a broadly accessible support store for
ninety days. Is the

85
00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:04.000
serving data boundary protected, and what
must change?

86
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:23.333
The boundary is not protected. Encryption
on the inference hop

87
00:07:23.333 --> 00:07:27.667
does not authorize a second copy in
telemetry. Stop exporting

88
00:07:27.667 --> 00:07:32.000
authorization headers and unjustified raw
content, revoke exposed credentials,

89
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.333
restrict the store, assess access, delete
existing copies under the

90
00:07:36.333 --> 00:07:40.667
incident and retention process, and verify
deletion. If approved content

91
00:07:40.667 --> 00:07:45.000
sampling is genuinely required, create a
separate minimized, redacted, encrypted,

92
00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:49.333
access-controlled, short-lived, audited
path. If your answer only encrypted the

93
00:07:49.333 --> 00:07:53.667
support store, revisit minimization.
Protection begins by not collecting what

94
00:07:53.667 --> 00:07:58.000
the operational question does not require.

95
00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.313
Measure latency and size as distributions
with suitable histogram resolution,

96
00:08:02.313 --> 00:08:06.625
not only averages. Preserve counts,
failures, and the population behind

97
00:08:06.625 --> 00:08:10.938
every percentile. Prometheus explains that
precomputed summary quantiles generally

98
00:08:10.938 --> 00:08:15.250
cannot be aggregated across replicas,
while compatible histogram buckets can

99
00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:19.563
be aggregated. Do not average three p95
values and call

100
00:08:19.563 --> 00:08:23.875
that a fleet p95. Aggregate the underlying
observations, then estimate

101
00:08:23.875 --> 00:08:28.188
once. Separate queue time, time to first
output, processing time,

102
00:08:28.188 --> 00:08:32.500
streaming duration, and total duration.
Track throughput, concurrency, rejections,

103
00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:36.813
cancellations, retries, cache behavior,
accelerator and host utilization, memory

104
00:08:36.813 --> 00:08:41.125
headroom, storage, network, and energy or
cloud charges where available.

105
00:08:41.125 --> 00:08:45.438
Use bounded workload, route, model,
release, and tenant-class dimensions.

106
00:08:45.438 --> 00:08:49.750
Kubernetes resource monitoring is general
deployment guidance, not proof that

107
00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:54.063
a particular deployment exports or
enforces a measurement. Attribute cost

108
00:08:54.063 --> 00:08:58.375
with explicit inputs and denominators.
State what the estimate includes

109
00:08:58.375 --> 00:09:02.688
and excludes. A number without its
population, window, and allocation

110
00:09:02.688 --> 00:09:07.000
rule is not decision-ready evidence.

111
00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:11.429
Work the supplied baseline. The bucket
bounds are 100, 200,

112
00:09:11.429 --> 00:09:15.857
400, and 800 milliseconds. Replica one has
cumulative counts 50,

113
00:09:15.857 --> 00:09:20.286
90, 100, 100. Replica two has 5, 15, 35,

114
00:09:20.286 --> 00:09:24.714
40. Replica three has 1, 2, 8, 10. Add

115
00:09:24.714 --> 00:09:29.143
matching buckets: 50 plus 5 plus 1 is 56;

116
00:09:29.143 --> 00:09:33.571
90 plus 15 plus 2 is 107; 100 plus 35

117
00:09:33.571 --> 00:09:38.000
plus 8 is 143; and 100 plus 40 plus 10

118
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.429
is 150. For p95, rank equals 0.95 times
150, or

119
00:09:42.429 --> 00:09:46.857
142.5. That rank lies in the 200-to-400
millisecond bucket. Linear

120
00:09:46.857 --> 00:09:51.286
interpolation gives 200 plus ((142.5 minus
107) divided by (143

121
00:09:51.286 --> 00:09:55.714
minus 107)) times 200, which is 397.22
milliseconds. This is

122
00:09:55.714 --> 00:10:00.143
an estimate, assuming observations are
spread within the bucket. The

123
00:10:00.143 --> 00:10:04.571
fixture has 143 of 150 requests at or
below 400

124
00:10:04.571 --> 00:10:09.000
milliseconds, or 95.333 percent, so it
passes a synthetic 95

125
00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.429
percent latency objective by about 0.333
percentage points. If two

126
00:10:13.429 --> 00:10:17.857
of the same 150 requests were
unsuccessful, 148 divided by

127
00:10:17.857 --> 00:10:22.286
150 equals 98.667 percent, which fails a
separate 99 percent

128
00:10:22.286 --> 00:10:26.714
success objective. Do not remove failures
from a denominator unless

129
00:10:26.714 --> 00:10:31.143
the indicator contract explicitly
justifies it. The synthetic cost example

130
00:10:31.143 --> 00:10:35.571
is three replicas for half an hour, at a

131
00:10:35.571 --> 00:10:40.000
fictional accelerator rate of two dollars
per replica-hour and fictional

132
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:44.429
host rate of forty cents per replica-hour.
Accelerator cost is

133
00:10:44.429 --> 00:10:48.857
3 times 0.5 times 2, or 3 dollars. Host

134
00:10:48.857 --> 00:10:53.286
cost is 3 times 0.5 times 0.40, or 0.60

135
00:10:53.286 --> 00:10:57.714
dollars. Add fictional storage of 0.05 and
network of 0.10

136
00:10:57.714 --> 00:11:02.143
to get 3.75 dollars. For 15,000 completed
requests, 3.75 divided

137
00:11:02.143 --> 00:11:06.571
by 15,000 is 0.00025 dollars per request.
These are teaching

138
00:11:06.571 --> 00:11:11.000
inputs, not provider prices or production
measurements.

139
00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:15.389
Alert on symptoms that require action:
user-visible objective burn, critical

140
00:11:15.389 --> 00:11:19.778
safety or security outcomes, sustained
queueing or rejection, memory pressure,

141
00:11:19.778 --> 00:11:24.167
unavailable replicas, exact identity
mismatch, telemetry loss, cost anomalies,

142
00:11:24.167 --> 00:11:28.556
and failed recovery checks. Every alert
needs a symptom, owner,

143
00:11:28.556 --> 00:11:32.944
severity, population, threshold and
duration, safe first action, runbook,

144
00:11:32.944 --> 00:11:37.333
evidence links, escalation, and recovery
condition. Five paper-tested examples are

145
00:11:37.333 --> 00:11:41.722
useful. A latency burn alert routes to
serving on-call and

146
00:11:41.722 --> 00:11:46.111
checks queueing and saturation before
scaling or rollback. A policy-deny

147
00:11:46.111 --> 00:11:50.500
anomaly routes to safety and security with
policy version and

148
00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:54.889
disposition counts, without content. A
queue alert fires only when

149
00:11:54.889 --> 00:11:59.278
p95 queue time and rejection rate are both
elevated for

150
00:11:59.278 --> 00:12:03.667
ten minutes. An identity mismatch alert
fires on any artifact,

151
00:12:03.667 --> 00:12:08.056
runtime, configuration, or release digest
outside the approved route manifest.

152
00:12:08.056 --> 00:12:12.444
A telemetry-loss or cost anomaly alert
includes missing-series evidence and

153
00:12:12.444 --> 00:12:16.833
allocation inputs. Test firing, routing,
deduplication, planned-maintenance

154
00:12:16.833 --> 00:12:21.222
suppression, escalation timers,
permissions, and recovery notifications.

155
00:12:21.222 --> 00:12:25.611
Missing telemetry is not proof of health.
Recovery must test

156
00:12:25.611 --> 00:12:30.000
affected outcomes, not merely the
existence of a rollback command.

157
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.455
Now build the privacy-conscious
observability contract. Write ten operator

158
00:12:34.455 --> 00:12:38.909
questions and map each to signals, exact
identities, bounded dimensions,

159
00:12:38.909 --> 00:12:43.364
owner, decision, and retention. Define
request, model, release, policy, latency,

160
00:12:43.364 --> 00:12:47.818
queue, error, saturation, and cost fields
with stability and cardinality

161
00:12:47.818 --> 00:12:52.273
limits. Classify every field as required
metadata, derived measurement, optional

162
00:12:52.273 --> 00:12:56.727
sensitive content, or prohibited secret.
Design latency and size histograms,

163
00:12:56.727 --> 00:13:01.182
objective calculations, and cost
allocation for three replicas without

164
00:13:01.182 --> 00:13:05.636
averaging quantiles. Create five alerts
and paper-test firing, routing, evidence,

165
00:13:05.636 --> 00:13:10.091
safe first action, runbook, escalation,
telemetry-loss behavior, and recovery.

166
00:13:10.091 --> 00:13:14.545
Your work is complete only when it answers
all ten

167
00:13:14.545 --> 00:13:19.000
questions and states field type,
stability, enum, range, and cardinality.

168
00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:23.400
Here is a worked contract. For approved
build, use startup

169
00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:27.800
inventory and request identity, exact
model, tokenizer, runtime, configuration,

170
00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:32.200
adapter, and release identities, with
environment, route, and release

171
00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:36.600
dimensions. The release owner isolates or
rolls back; example retention

172
00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:41.000
is 90 days for inventory and 30 days for

173
00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:45.400
request metadata. For valid outcomes, use
status and outcome counters

174
00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:49.800
by route, operation, model, release, and
bounded outcome; product reliability

175
00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:54.200
staff retain or halt a release for 30
days. For

176
00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:58.600
a failing reviewed slice, use evaluation
and outcome counters with

177
00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:03.000
evaluation-set version, release, bounded
locale, route, and workload class; the

178
00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:07.400
evaluation lead blocks promotion or
commissions tests for 90 days.

179
00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:11.800
For time spent, use gateway, queue,
first-output, processing, tool, and

180
00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:16.200
end-to-end histograms plus request-linked
traces. The performance owner tunes the

181
00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:20.600
measured stage; metrics remain 90 days and
sampled traces 7

182
00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:25.000
days. For queued or rejected work, use
queue histograms, depth

183
00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:29.400
gauges, and rejection counters with
bounded rejection reasons; the capacity

184
00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:33.800
owner shapes traffic or adds reviewed
capacity for 30 days.

185
00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:38.200
For saturation, use accelerator and host
utilization, memory headroom, storage,

186
00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:42.600
and network activity; the infrastructure
owner changes placement or limits

187
00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:47.000
for 30 days. For quality or security
change, retain before-and-after

188
00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:51.400
outcomes, policy and evaluation fixtures,
old and new exact bundles,

189
00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:55.800
policy version, bounded test suite, and
disposition for 180 days.

190
00:17:55.800 --> 00:18:00.200
For cost change, use metered resource time
and charge allocation

191
00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:04.600
by workload, route, model, release, and
bounded tenant class; finance

192
00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:09.000
operations investigates for 13 months
subject to policy. For recovery,

193
00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:13.400
use backup, restore, rollback, and probe
events with elapsed-time histograms;

194
00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:17.800
the resilience owner accepts or remediates
for 180 days. For

195
00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:22.200
incident scope and reproduction, correlate
governed traces, deployment events,

196
00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:26.600
deterministic probes, request or run
identifiers, exact bundles, route, status,

197
00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:31.000
and bounded error category under the
approved incident schedule. The

198
00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:35.400
field contract is obs-contract/v1. The
request identifier is required application

199
00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:39.800
metadata and prohibited as a metric label.
Build digests and

200
00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:44.200
release identifiers have at most 20 active
values per environment.

201
00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:48.600
Policy disposition is limited to allow,
deny, review, error, and

202
00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:53.000
unknown, with at most 20 active policy
versions. Latency and

203
00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:57.400
queue measurements are finite from 0
through 3,600,000 milliseconds and

204
00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:01.800
use configured buckets. Error category is
limited to none, timeout,

205
00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:06.200
overload, invalid request, policy,
dependency, internal, and unknown.

206
00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:10.600
Saturation is finite from 0 through 1.
Allocated cost is

207
00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:15.000
finite and nonnegative. Unknown enums and
types are rejected or

208
00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:19.400
mapped explicitly to unknown at the
adapter boundary. Cardinality alarms

209
00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:23.800
compare active series with the contract
budget. Privacy review covers

210
00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:28.200
access, retention, redaction, encryption,
sampling, deletion, audit, and support

211
00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:32.600
bundles. Deny-all, removed validation, or
an unowned dashboard does not

212
00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:37.000
pass.

213
00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:41.500
Before seeing the answer, inspect
changed.json. Write down its cumulative

214
00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:46.000
bucket totals and predict whether
traffic-weighted aggregation will be

215
00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:50.500
higher or lower than equal averaging of
replica p95 estimates.

216
00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:55.000
Explain your prediction using the fact
that replica three carries

217
00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:59.500
25 requests and places most of them in the

218
00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:04.000
first two buckets. Then state the
postcondition your repair must

219
00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:08.500
preserve: validation remains active, all
